<header><p class="st-kicker">protectorate · North America and Oceania</p><h1 class="st-title">British Solomon Islands Protectorate</h1><p class="st-meta">1893 CE – 1978 CE · capital Tulagi; later Honiara</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">A British protectorate in the Solomon Islands for 85 years, scene of the Guadalcanal campaign and of the Maasina Rule movement, now the Solomon Islands.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>To control the labour recruitment trade to <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/colony-of-queensland">Queensland</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fiji">Fiji</a>, and in competition with <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/german-new-guinea">German New Guinea</a>, <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/united-kingdom">Britain</a> declared a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/protectorate">protectorate</a> over the southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/solomon">Solomons</a> in 1893. It was extended in 1898 and 1899, and in 1900 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a> transferred <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/santa-isabel-island">Santa Isabel</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/choiseul-island">Choiseul</a> and other northern islands to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-woodford">Charles Woodford</a> became the first Resident Commissioner at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tulagi">Tulagi</a> in 1896.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The administration was tiny and relied on head taxes and on plantation companies such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/levers-pacific-plantations">Lever's Pacific Plantations</a> to develop copra. Christian missions spread widely. Resistance continued, notably on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/malaita">Malaita</a>, where <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kwaio">Kwaio</a> warriors killed the district officer <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/william-r-bell">William Bell</a> in 1927. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a> invaded in 1942, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guadalcanal-campaign">Guadalcanal campaign</a> of 1942 and 1943 turned the war in the Pacific. After the war the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maasina-rule">Maasina Rule</a> movement on Malaita challenged British authority.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>The capital moved to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/honiara">Honiara</a>, built on the wartime American base. A legislative council and self-government followed, and the islands became independent as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/solomon">Solomon</a> Islands on 7 July 1978, with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peter-kenilorea">Peter Kenilorea</a> as prime minister.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/british-solomon-islands-protectorate.jpg" alt="British Solomon Islands Protectorate" width="320" height="160"><figcaption>Image: Thommy (original) · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_the_Solomon_Islands_(1966%E2%80%931977).svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1893 CE – 1978 CE</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>protectorate</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>colonial</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Tulagi; later Honiara</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Charles Woodford, Peter Kenilorea</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Protestantism, Roman Catholicism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>English, Solomon Islands Pijin</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd>Solomon Islands</dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>British Solomon Islands</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1936&amp;focus=british-solomon-islands-protectorate" aria-label="British Solomon Islands Protectorate on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="9102 2517 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where British Solomon Islands Protectorate lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M9422 2773L4997 651M9422 2773L4997 651M9422 2773L8529 1145"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M4997 651h0M4997 651h0M8529 1145h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M9422 2773h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M9422 2773h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Tulagi. <a href="/atlas?year=1936&amp;focus=british-solomon-islands-protectorate">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>

